        $drafts = [
            ['Finding the Right School for Your Child: A Guide for Every Parent', 'finding-the-right-school-for-your-child', 'parent-family-guides', 'A practical guide to comparing learning environments, location, programmes, facilities and school costs.', '<p>Choosing a school is an important decision. The right learning environment should support a child’s academic, social and personal development while fitting the practical needs of the family.</p><h2>Start with your child’s needs</h2><p>Consider your child’s age, interests, strengths, learning preferences and any additional support they may need. A school that suits one child may not be the best fit for another. Think about the environment in which your child feels safe, encouraged and ready to learn.</p><h2>Look at the learning environment</h2><p>Pay attention to classroom organisation, cleanliness, safety, teacher-learner interaction and the general atmosphere. Ask how learners are supported when they need extra guidance and how the school communicates progress to parents.</p><h2>Check programmes and facilities</h2><p>Find out which education levels, subjects and programmes are offered. Consider libraries, laboratories, computer rooms, playgrounds, sports spaces and creative facilities. Ask how learners actually use these resources.</p><h2>Think about location and cost</h2><p>Consider travel time, transport arrangements and whether the location is practical for your household. Ask for a clear explanation of tuition and other costs such as meals, uniforms, learning materials, boarding and school activities. Request current figures directly from the institution.</p><h2>Visit and compare</h2><p>Where possible, arrange a school visit. Ask about class sizes, teaching approaches, learner welfare, extracurricular activities and admission requirements. Compare more than one school before deciding.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>The right school is not necessarily the biggest, closest or most famous. It is the institution whose learning environment, programmes and practical arrangements fit your child’s needs and your family’s circumstances. Use FindURSCH to discover schools and organise your shortlist, then confirm details directly with each institution.</p>'],
            ['Looking for Daycare or Kindergarten? What Parents Should Know', 'looking-for-daycare-or-kindergarten', 'early-childhood-daycare', 'Understand the difference between daycare and kindergarten and what to look for in an early learning environment.', '<p>Choosing a daycare or kindergarten is often the beginning of a child’s educational journey. Young children need an environment where they feel comfortable, protected and encouraged to explore.</p><h2>Daycare and kindergarten</h2><p>Daycare generally focuses on care and supervision while parents are working or attending to other responsibilities. Depending on the centre, it may also include play and early learning activities. Kindergarten places more emphasis on preparing children for formal education through age-appropriate communication, early literacy, numeracy, social interaction and creative activities. Some institutions provide both services, so confirm exactly what is offered.</p><h2>Look for a safe environment</h2><p>Observe the condition of rooms and play areas. Ask how children are supervised, how visitors are managed, what happens in an emergency and how collection and drop-off are handled. Cleanliness, safe equipment and clear routines matter.</p><h2>Meet the caregivers</h2><p>Ask about staff experience, training, supervision arrangements and how many children are assigned to each caregiver. Young children benefit from patient adults who communicate clearly and understand that children develop at different rates.</p><h2>Ask about learning through play</h2><p>Drawing, storytelling, music, building blocks, movement and group play encourage children to explore ideas and practise new skills. Ask what a typical day looks like and how activities are adapted for different ages.</p><h2>Keep communication open</h2><p>Find out about meals, rest, hygiene and how individual needs are handled. Ask how the centre shares updates about a child’s day and whether parents can arrange a visit before enrolment.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>Look for a centre that respects children’s individual needs, provides reliable care and creates opportunities to learn, play and build early relationships. Confirm details directly before enrolling.</p>'],
            ['Preparing Your Child for the First Day of Primary School', 'preparing-your-child-for-primary-school', 'primary-education', 'Practical ways to help children feel confident and settle into new school routines.', '<p>Starting primary school is an exciting milestone. It brings new routines, friendships, teachers and opportunities to learn. The transition can also feel unfamiliar, especially for children moving from home or kindergarten into a more structured environment.</p><h2>Talk positively about school</h2><p>Explain what a school day may involve, such as meeting teachers, learning with classmates, playing and taking part in activities. Encourage your child to ask questions and talk about worries. Focus on helping them feel supported.</p><h2>Establish a daily routine</h2><p>Gradually practise regular waking, mealtimes, reading and bedtime routines before school begins. A predictable schedule can make mornings less stressful and help children become familiar with the structure of a school day.</p><h2>Encourage independence</h2><p>Give children opportunities to organise simple belongings, put away toys, communicate their needs and practise basic self-care. These responsibilities can build confidence while adults remain available to support them.</p><h2>Prepare requirements early</h2><p>Confirm the school’s actual requirements for uniforms, books, stationery, meals and transport. Prepare the school bag together and check the start time and travel arrangements.</p><h2>Stay involved</h2><p>Ask open questions about the child’s day and listen without rushing to correct or judge. Keep in touch with teachers and pay attention to changes in mood, confidence or routine. Some children settle quickly, while others need more time.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>A successful start involves more than a uniform and school bag. Children need reassurance, patient guidance and encouragement as they adjust to a new environment.</p>'],
            ['Exploring Primary Schools in Uganda: What Makes a Good Learning Environment?', 'exploring-primary-schools-in-uganda', 'primary-education', 'Questions parents can ask about teaching, facilities, learner support and activities beyond the classroom.', '<p>Primary education gives children opportunities to build knowledge, develop friendships, discover interests and establish learning habits. For parents exploring primary schools in Uganda, the search should go beyond finding an institution close to home.</p><h2>Look beyond the school name</h2><p>Consider the classroom environment, teaching approach, available facilities and how the school supports learners who need additional attention. A school’s reputation alone does not explain the everyday learning experience.</p><h2>Ask how learning is organised</h2><p>Find out how lessons are delivered, how learners are assessed and how teachers communicate progress. Ask what support is available when a child struggles with a topic and how the school encourages reading, problem-solving and participation.</p><h2>Understand the facilities</h2><p>Libraries can support reading and independent learning. Laboratories and practical learning spaces help learners explore ideas. Playgrounds and sports facilities encourage movement and teamwork. Ask which facilities are available and how learners use them.</p><h2>Explore activities beyond lessons</h2><p>Sports, music, clubs, creative activities and group projects can help children develop confidence, cooperation and communication. Find out which activities are offered and how learners are encouraged to participate.</p><h2>Compare schools fairly</h2><p>Create a shortlist using the factors that matter most to your family: location, programmes, facilities, fees, transport, learning environment and learner support. Request current information and arrange visits where possible.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>A good learning environment helps children feel safe, supported and engaged. FindURSCH can help you discover institutions and organise your comparison. Speak directly with the schools you are considering to confirm details.</p>'],
            ['Boarding or Day School? Understanding the Options for Your Child', 'boarding-or-day-school', 'secondary-education', 'Compare boarding and day school options by considering distance, welfare, learner readiness and total costs.', '<p>When choosing a secondary school, families may need to decide whether a boarding school or day school is the better option. The right choice depends on a student’s needs, family circumstances and the school itself.</p><h2>Day schools</h2><p>Day schools allow learners to attend classes and return home after the school day. This can give families more opportunities to remain involved in daily routines and may be practical where a suitable school is accessible within a manageable distance.</p><h2>Boarding schools</h2><p>Boarding schools provide accommodation within the school environment. The experience may encourage independence and structured routines. Parents should understand accommodation, supervision, meals, healthcare, student welfare and communication arrangements.</p><h2>Think about the learner</h2><p>Consider the student’s maturity, comfort, health needs and ability to adapt to a residential environment. Talk through the options with the learner and give them room to express concerns.</p><h2>Compare the full cost</h2><p>Request current fee information and ask what is included. Boarding may involve accommodation and meals, while day schooling may involve transport and other daily expenses. Compare total costs rather than tuition alone.</p><h2>Ask about student welfare</h2><p>Find out how the school handles supervision, discipline, healthcare, safeguarding, pastoral support and student complaints. Ask how parents can communicate with staff and receive updates.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>There is no single option that suits every student. Consider learning experience, welfare, family routine and total cost. Visit schools and confirm arrangements before applying.</p>'],
            ['Choosing the Right University Course: Start With Your Interests and Goals', 'choosing-the-right-university-course', 'higher-education', 'A student guide to researching programmes, entry requirements, learning experiences and career options.', '<p>Choosing a university course is a major step towards higher education and future career opportunities. It helps to approach the process with research and realistic expectations.</p><h2>Begin with your interests</h2><p>Think about the subjects and activities that interest you. You may be drawn to technology, business, science, education, healthcare, agriculture, engineering or creative fields. Your interests can help identify areas worth exploring.</p><h2>Research entry requirements</h2><p>Different programmes have different requirements. Review the institution’s current published requirements and confirm which qualifications and subject combinations are needed. Contact the university when anything is unclear.</p><h2>Understand the course</h2><p>Read the programme description and course structure where available. Find out the balance between lectures, practical work, research, projects, placements and assessments. Ask whether the programme includes opportunities to develop professional or technical skills.</p><h2>Compare institutions</h2><p>Consider programme availability, location, tuition, other study expenses, learning facilities, accommodation, student welfare and career-development opportunities. Confirm current fees and deadlines directly with the institution.</p><h2>Think about careers realistically</h2><p>Research roles associated with a qualification, but remember that a course does not automatically guarantee employment. Practical experience, communication, problem-solving, digital skills and continued learning can also matter.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>The university decision should be informed by research, not pressure. Take time to compare programmes and choose a path that fits your interests, abilities and circumstances.</p>'],
            ['Beyond the Classroom: Why Extracurricular Activities Matter', 'why-extracurricular-activities-matter', 'student-development', 'Discover how sports, arts, clubs and student activities can help learners build confidence and practical skills.', '<p>School is not only about lessons, assignments and examinations. Activities outside the classroom give learners opportunities to discover interests, develop talents and interact with others in different settings.</p><h2>Learning teamwork through sports</h2><p>Sports can help learners practise cooperation, discipline, commitment and fair play. They also provide opportunities for physical activity and healthy competition when organised safely and inclusively.</p><h2>Building confidence through communication</h2><p>Debate, drama, public speaking and student leadership activities allow learners to express ideas, listen to others and communicate with different audiences.</p><h2>Developing creativity through the arts</h2><p>Music, drawing, photography, writing and other creative activities encourage imagination and self-expression. They can also teach learners how to practise a skill, receive feedback and complete a project.</p><h2>Discovering practical interests</h2><p>Science clubs, technology activities, environmental projects and entrepreneurship programmes introduce learners to practical problem-solving and new interests. These experiences can help students connect classroom ideas to everyday situations.</p><h2>Finding a healthy balance</h2><p>Extracurricular activities should complement learning rather than overwhelm it. Consider a learner’s interests, time, wellbeing and academic responsibilities. Schools should provide age-appropriate activities with clear supervision.</p><h2>Final thoughts</h2><p>A student’s development includes more than academic results. Opportunities to participate, collaborate and discover new interests can contribute to confidence and personal growth.</p>'],
